###############################################################
# scPred Model Training and Prediction
###############################################################

This project includes scripts for training an scPred model using Tabula Sapiens data and predicting cell labels on a query dataset using the pre-trained model.

###############################################################
## Dependencies
###############################################################
R packages: scPred, Seurat, magrittr, doParallel, cowplot, ComplexHeatmap

These packages have been installed on the Rstudio server, so that you could directly run the scripts on server.

The software versions of the installed R packages when the author performed test run:
scPred (version 1.9.2)
Seurat (version 4.4.0)
magrittr (version 2.0.3)
doParallel (version 1.0.17)
cowplot (version 1.1.1)
ComplexHeatmap (version 2.14.0)

###############################################################
## Project Structure
###############################################################
scRNAseq_cell_type_classification/
│
├── train_model.sh           # Shell script to train the model
├── predict_cell_types_using_pretrained_model.rmd        # R Markdown file to predict cell labels using the pre-trained model
├── predict_cell_types_using_pretrained_model.html        # HTML file generated by knitting predict_cell_types_using_pretrained_model.rmd 
├── predict_cell_types_using_pretrained_model.R     # R script file to run directly through command line (the content same as the Rmd file)
├── scPred_Tabula_sapiens.R        # R script called by train_model.sh to train a model
├── scPred_Tabula_muris.R        # R script to train models on Tabula muris data (mouse)
│
├── reference_data/
│   ├── developing_brain   # Reference data for human brains at early developmental stages
│       ├── developing_brain.rds   # Seurat object with cell label annotation
│       ├── human_developing_brain_scPred_model.rds # pre-trained scPred model of developing brain
│       ├── scPred_default_model_performance_HumanDevBrain.pdf # the plot showing performance evaluation of the model above
│       ├── README   # Documentation file containing the source to download the data
│   ├── Tabula_sapiens   # Reference data of Tabula sapiens (human) (using Ensembl IDs)
│       ├── All_cells.rds   # Seurat object with cell label annotation 
│       ├── model/ # The pre-trained model built by scPred
│           ├── Tabula_sapiens_downsample100_scPred_default_model.rds   # The pre-trained model file
│       ├── README   # Documentation file containing the source to download the data
│   ├── Tabula_muris   # Reference data of Tabula muris (mouse) (using gene symbol as IDs)
│       ├── 10x   # count matrix files and cell annotation files from Tabula muris data using 10x Genomics platform
│       ├── SMART-seq # The pre-trained model built by scPred
│           ├── count_matrix/   # The count matrix and metadata of Tabula muris SMART-seq data
│           ├── Seurat_objects/   # The Seurat objects built from count matrix 
│           ├── pre-trained_models/   # The pre-trained model built by scPred, including models for all tissues and for each tissue
│           ├── scPred_default_model_performance.pdf  # The plot regarding performance evaluation of the model
│           ├── Tabula_Muris_SMART-seq_tissue_list.txt  # The complete list of available tissues in Tabula muris SMART-seq dataset
│           ├── README  # The download link of the Tabula muris SMART-seq data, as well as file descriptions in the current folder
│
├── sample_dataset/  # sample dataset to perform test run
│   ├── human_lung_seurat_object_ENSEMBL_ID.rds               # Seurat object with curated cell annoation from human lung cell atlas 
│   └── README      # Documentation file containing the source to download the data
│
├── test_run/    # sample result files generated by test run using the sample dataset
│   ├── test_run_model.rds   # output model by train_model.sh
│   ├── human_lung_cell_type_scpred_prediction.csv   # The table containing predicted cell label for each cell in sample dataset
│   ├── human_lung_cell_type_true_labels.csv     # The original cell type annotation of sample dataset as 'ground truth'
│   ├── scPred_default_model_performance.pdf     # performance evaluation plot generated by scPred
│   ├── compare_human_lung_true_labels_with_scpred.pdf   # UMAP plots showing true labels and predict labels of cell types in a side-by-side manner
│   ├── confusion_matrix_heatmap.pdf    # Heatmap showing predicted labels as rows and true labels as columns for comparison
│   ├── scPred_cell_type_prediction_score_matrix.rds    # saved data matrix containing the probability score of each cell predicted as a certain cell type
│
└── README                # Documentation file

##############################################################
## Usage
##############################################################

### 1. Prepare your reference dataset

This script takes a Seurat object of scRNA-seq data as reference to train a model. 

You could download the well annotated Seurat object from public resources like Tabula sapiens, or create your own Seurat object and save it using readRDS() in R.

For your convenience, we have downloaded the Seurat object of Tabula sapiens data. You could find it at reference_data/Tabula_sapiens/local.rds. 
Tabula sapiens data was downloaded from:
https://cellxgene.cziscience.com/collections/e5f58829-1a66-40b5-a624-9046778e74f5

Please note that this Seurat object downloaded from Tabula sapiens uses Ensembl ID as their gene ids. If you would like to align your query data to the reference, please make sure both the query and reference data use the same type of gene ids.

### 2. Train the scPred Model

Run the `train_model.sh` script to train the scPred model using Tabula Sapiens data.

```bash
/nfs/BaRC_Public/BaRC_code/R/scRNAseq_cell_type_classification/train_model.sh -r reference_data/Tabula_sapiens/local.rds -o test_run/ -m test_run_model.rds -d 100 -n 16 -mem 64G
```

When you run this command line, it will send a job to the slurm cluster. Depending on the size of the dataset, the job might take several hours to finish. Meanwhile, you could check the status of the job by:

squeue -u your_user_name

This will give you information about the job id, running time and status of the current job.

Note: -d parameter is set to NULL by default to use all the cells in the reference to train the model. However, we recommend to downsample the data to speed up the training process. It is proved that using downsampled data can achieve very good performance on cell type prediction. The suggested downsample size can be 100~500.

### 3. Pre-trained models

We have provided pre-trained models built by the script above, based on Tabula sapiens (for human) and Tabula muris (for mouse) datasets.

The human pre-trained model is located at: ./reference_data/Tabula_sapiens/model
The mouse pre-trained models is located at: ./reference_data/Tabula_muris/SMART-seq/pre-trained_models/

IMPORTANT: Please note that Tabula sapiens use Ensembl IDs while Tabula muris use gene symbols, please make sure the ID types of your query Seurat object is consistent with the ID types of the pre-trained model.

Note: Currently, we used SMART-seq data from Tabula muris to train the models, because it includes more tissues of interest. Later on, I may also train models based on 10x datasets.

### 4. Predict Cell Labels
1) use Rmd file in the Rstudio environment
The user needs to specify the input and output files at the top of the file named "predict_cell_types_using_pretrained_model.rmd".
Provide the correct paths for the pre-trained model and query data. Also specify the file names of the output figures and tables. 
Then, knit the R Markdown document to predict cell labels.

2) use R script file in the command line
The user needs to edit the input and output files at the top of the file named "predict_cell_types_using_pretrained_model.R".
Provide the correct paths for the pre-trained model and query data. Also specify the file names of the output figures and tables. 
Then, execute the R script in the terminal:
Rscript ./predict_cell_types_using_pretrained_model.R
The user could submit the job to the slurm cluster by using 'srun' or 'sbatch' (highly recommended!).

For a full documentation of the usage of scPred, please refer to https://github.com/powellgenomicslab/scPred.
